---
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import type { StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const expert = view.mode === "expert";
const claim = (id: string) => {
  const evidence = view.evidence.find((e) => e.claimId === `luong-2015-${id}`);
  if (!evidence) throw new Error(`Missing Luong evidence: ${id}`);
  return evidence;
};
const englishGerman = [
  ["Buck et al. · phrase-based + large language model", "—", "20.7"],
  ["Jean et al. · RNNsearch", "—", "16.5"],
  ["Jean et al. · RNNsearch + unknown replacement", "—", "19.0"],
  ["Jean et al. · + unknown replacement + large vocabulary + eight-model ensemble", "—", "21.6"],
  ["Base", "10.6", "11.3"], ["Base + reversal", "9.9", "12.6", "+1.3"],
  ["Base + reversal + dropout", "8.1", "14.0", "+1.4"],
  ["Base + reversal + dropout + global/location", "7.3", "16.8", "+2.8"],
  ["Base + reversal + dropout + global/location + input feeding", "6.4", "18.1", "+1.3"],
  ["Base + reversal + dropout + local-p/general + input feeding", "5.9", "19.0", "+0.9"],
  ["Same local-p system + unknown replacement", "—", "20.9", "+1.9"],
  ["Heterogeneous eight-model ensemble + unknown replacement", "—", "23.0", "+2.1"],
];
const germanEnglish = [
  ["Edinburgh · phrase-based", "—", "29.2"], ["MILA · NMT + 5-gram reranking", "—", "27.6"],
  ["Base with source reversal", "14.3", "16.9"],
  ["+ global/location", "12.7", "19.1", "+2.2"],
  ["+ global/location + input feeding", "10.9", "20.1", "+1.0"],
  ["+ global/dot + dropout + input feeding", "9.7", "22.8", "+2.7"],
  ["Same global/dot system + unknown replacement", "—", "24.9", "+2.1"],
];
const sweep = [
  ["global/location", "6.4", "18.1", "19.3"], ["global/dot", "6.1", "18.6", "20.5"],
  ["global/general", "6.1", "17.3", "19.1"], ["local-m/dot · two runs", ">7.0", "×", "×"],
  ["local-m/general", "6.2", "18.6", "20.4"], ["local-p/dot", "6.6", "18.0", "19.6"],
  ["local-p/general", "5.9", "19.0", "20.9"],
];
const alignment = [["global/location", "0.39"], ["local-m/general", "0.34"], ["local-p/general", "0.36"], ["Ensemble", "0.34"], ["Berkeley Aligner", "0.32"]];
---
<section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="luong-results-heading"><p class="kicker">Complete result record · proceedings Tables 1–4 and 6</p><h2 id="luong-results-heading">Read the ingredients before the headline.</h2><p>BLEU evaluates translation wording against references; larger is better under the same conditions. Perplexity (Ppl) evaluates reference-token prediction; smaller is better. A dash means unreported, never zero. Parenthesized changes below are the paper’s printed consecutive-row gains. Table 5 contains selected translations, not an aggregate score table.</p></section>
<section class="reading-block alignment-result-section" aria-labelledby="luong-en-de-heading"><p class="kicker">Table 1 · WMT’14 English → German</p><h2 id="luong-en-de-heading">Five BLEU points change several components.</h2><p>All twelve rows use newstest2014 and case-sensitive tokenized BLEU. The 14.0 → 19.0 comparison adds attention, changes its scope and score function, and adds input feeding. The 18.1 → 19.0 step changes global/location to local-p/general. Neither difference isolates locality alone.</p><table class="trace-table luong-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 1 · English-to-German component ladder</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Ppl</th><th scope="col">BLEU</th></tr></thead><tbody>{englishGerman.map(([label, ppl, bleu, gain]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{ppl}</td><td>{bleu}{gain && <small class="luong-gain">({gain})</small>}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">The 23.0 endpoint ensembles eight models with different attention/dropout settings and uses unknown-word replacement. Replacement is a separate decoding addition. The Jean comparison rows concern Jean’s system, rather than the original Bahdanau experiment in the preceding chapter.</p><details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>{expert ? "Audit the five-point comparison" : "Check whether five points measure locality alone"}</summary><p>They do not. The 14.0 baseline already has reversal and dropout; the 19.0 endpoint adds local-p/general attention and input feeding. Even the 0.9 step changes both scope and score. Unknown replacement and heterogeneous ensembling then produce separate 20.9 and 23.0 endpoints.</p></details><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("english-german-results")} /></section>
<div class="alignment-evidence">
  <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-wmt15-heading"><p class="kicker">Table 2 · WMT’15 English → German</p><h2 id="luong-wmt15-heading">A different test and BLEU script.</h2><table class="trace-table luong-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 2 · newstest2015 NIST BLEU</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">BLEU</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">MILA · NMT + 5-gram reranking</th><td>24.9</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Heterogeneous eight-model ensemble + unknown replacement</th><td>25.9</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="mt-3">Both are NIST BLEU. The one-point difference is a same-table comparison; subtracting Table 1’s 23.0 from 25.9 mixes test sets and metric scripts. The models still train on WMT’14 data.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("wmt15-ensemble-result")} /></section>
  <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-de-en-heading"><p class="kicker">Table 3 · WMT’15 German → English</p><h2 id="luong-de-en-heading">The reverse direction is not state of the art.</h2><p>The table reports WMT’15 German-to-English BLEU. Its caption refers back to Table 1 but does not independently identify the script or test filename; §4 names both tokenized and NIST evaluation. Keep these rows under their reported table condition rather than pooling them with Tables 1 or 2. The base already includes source reversal; each neural label names the components added to that base.</p><table class="trace-table luong-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 3 · German-to-English component ladder</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Ppl</th><th scope="col">BLEU</th></tr></thead><tbody>{germanEnglish.map(([label, ppl, bleu, gain]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{ppl}</td><td>{bleu}{gain && <small class="luong-gain">({gain})</small>}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">24.9 includes unknown replacement and remains below both listed comparison systems. The paper explicitly declines a state-of-the-art claim in this direction.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("german-english-results")} /></section>
</div>
<div class="alignment-evidence">
  <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-sweep-heading"><p class="kicker">Table 4 and footnote 15 · English → German</p><h2 id="luong-sweep-heading">Missing runs constrain the ranking.</h2><p>Selected newstest2014 architectures; tokenized BLEU before and after unknown replacement. × reproduces the paper’s missing BLEU entries for two unsuccessful local-m/dot runs. It is not a score of zero.</p><table class="trace-table luong-sweep mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 4 · score and source-scope sweep</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Architecture</th><th scope="col">Ppl</th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">After</th></tr></thead><tbody>{sweep.map(([label, ppl, before, after]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{ppl}</td><td>{before}</td><td>{after}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Printed unknown-replacement gains, in row order excluding the failed row: +1.2, +1.9, +1.8, +1.8, +1.9, +1.9. For local-p/dot, the printed +1.9 conflicts with 19.6 − 18.0 = 1.6. The values and discrepancy are retained rather than silently repaired.</p><table class="trace-table luong-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical footnote 15 · concat perplexities only</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Architecture</th><th scope="col">Ppl</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">global/concat</th><td>6.7</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">local-m/concat</th><td>7.1</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">local-p/concat</th><td>7.1</td></tr></tbody></table><p class="mt-3">No concat BLEU is reported. Later v5 footnote 15 additionally says its concat implementation fixes the source-state block of Wₐ to identity; it offers that simplification as a possible explanation, not a demonstrated cause of the high perplexities. The authors cite resource limits for the incomplete combination sweep. A selected best row does not establish a universal ordering of score families.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("attention-architecture-results")} /></section>
  <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="luong-aer-heading"><p class="kicker">Table 6 · RWTH Europarl alignment evaluation</p><h2 id="luong-aer-heading">Alignment error measures a different task.</h2><p>On 508 sentence pairs with gold links, the models are forced to follow reference translations. The procedure selects one source link per target word from the largest attention weight. Alignment error rate (AER) is lower when the extracted links agree better with this gold annotation.</p><table class="trace-table luong-results mt-4"><caption>Canonical Table 6 · alignment error rates</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Method</th><th scope="col">AER</th></tr></thead><tbody>{alignment.map(([label, value]) => <tr><th scope="row">{label}</th><td>{value}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table><p class="mt-3">Berkeley is trained on the 508 pairs plus one million WMT pairs and permits one-to-many links. Its data and extraction conditions differ from the neural systems. The ensemble ties local-m on AER despite its stronger translation result; lower AER is not interchangeable with higher BLEU or a faithful explanation.</p><details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>{expert ? "Audit which target word receives the link" : "Check what this alignment test establishes"}</summary><p>{expert ? "Proceedings footnote 14 attaches aₜ to predicted yₜ for location scoring but to input yₜ₋₁ for content scoring. This is a link-extraction convention, not a change to the current-state prediction path. Forced decoding, that convention and one-link selection must be retained when comparing AER." : "The model is following the reference, not generating its own translation. The selected link is evaluated against gold word links under a specific protocol. This does not prove that a free-running translation is correct or that the attention map explains every model decision."}</p></details><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Link timing matters: footnote 14 uses aₜ for predicted yₜ with location scoring, but for input yₜ₋₁ with content scoring. This does not move the model’s target-vocabulary prediction to a different step.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("alignment-evaluation")} /></section>
</div>
